      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let $a_n = \\dfrac{2n+1}{x-n}$. Summing yields \\[ \\dfrac{2}{x-1} + \\dfrac{4}{x-2} + \\dfrac{6}{x-3} + \\dfrac{8}{x-4} + \\dfrac{10}{x-5} + \\dfrac{12}{x-6} + \\dfrac{14}{x-7} + \\dfrac{16}{x-8} + \\dfrac{18}{x-9} + \\dfrac{20}{x-10} = x^2 - 11x - 4 = a_0 a_{10}.\\]Multiplying through by $x-10$, we have \\[2x+20-10+x(x^2-11x-4)-a_0 a_{10} (x-10) = a_0 a_{10} x-10a_{10}.\\]Then $a_0 a_{10} (x-10) = 10$. Since $a_0$ and $a_{10}$ are positive, they are the two positive zeros of the quadratic, and thus the sum of both of them is 10+5=15 by Vieta's formulas. The radical square can come from 2, 4, 6, or 8, which we can get from 2-6, 4-8, 6-2, or 8-4 respectively. Adding the radicands for all cases, we get 67+108+108+108 = 400. Therefore, $a+b+c = \\boxed{400}.$. The answer is 400",
